{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1583749884/17/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ac6f9922\" lines=\"18-40\">\n<description>\nSummarize the enclosed table slots by the single object color inside each slot, using the slot background color for empty slots.\n\nInput structure\n- The most common color forms the outer background and all enclosed slot\n  backgrounds.\n- The enclosed background components form a complete rectangular array of\n  slots, ordered by their positions.\n- Each slot contains either no other color or exactly one object color.\n\nRule\n- Find all 4-connected components of the most common color that do not touch\n  the input edge.\n- Sort their bounding boxes into row and column order to define the output\n  table.\n- For each slot, output its unique non-background color if present; otherwise\n  output the background color.\n\nKey insight\n- The large grid is an expanded table: the enclosed background components are\n  the cells, and any colored object drawn inside a cell is that cell's summary\n  value.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"5623160b\" lines=\"44-59\">\n<description>\nPush every colored object touching maroon markers straight outward until it reaches the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Maroon pixels are fixed markers.\n- Every other foreground 4-connected object touches the marker pixels on exactly one cardinal side.\n\nRule:\n- Clear each non-background, non-maroon object from its original position.\n- Translate it in the direction away from its maroon contact until the object reaches the grid boundary.\n- Keep the maroon marker pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The maroon contact side is a launcher: it determines a unique outward ray for the whole object, and the edge of the grid determines the translation distance.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"221dfab4\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nPropagate a repeating striped pattern from a yellow border segment toward the opposite edge, recoloring foreground objects on certain stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Foreground blobs of another color scattered in the interior.\n- A short yellow (4) segment sits on one border edge.\n\nRule:\n- From the yellow segment, march toward the opposite edge in steps of 2 cells, cycling colors Y, Y, G (4, 4, 3). The 1-cell gap between stripes is forced to bg.\n- Yellow stripes paint only within the segment's span.\n- Green stripes additionally recolor all non-bg pixels across the entire row/column to green.\n\nKey insight: Green stripes act as a \"flood\" that recolors existing objects on the full row/column, while yellow stripes are confined to the segment span.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d6542281\" lines=\"72-91\">\n<description>\nComplete each partial marker mask by stamping the matching multicolor sprite over it.\n\nInput structure\n- A dominant background contains one or more 8-connected multicolor sprites.\n- Separate target masks use colors that already occur inside one of those sprites.\n- A target mask may contain only one marker pixel or several same-alignment marker pixels.\n\nRule\n- For each multicolor sprite, test every translation that aligns a target pixel with a\n  same-color sprite pixel.\n- Keep the translations with maximal exact overlap against the existing target pixels,\n  rejecting translations that conflict with other foreground or overlap a source sprite.\n- Stamp the whole sprite at those translations; if a non-marker sprite color occurs as\n  a single source pixel, consume that source-only marker back to background.\n\nKey insight\nThe scattered target pixels are partial copies: their same-color overlap with a sprite\ndetermines where the full sprite must be completed.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"412b6263\" lines=\"39-54\">\n<description>\nInfer the background, rotate the whole input 90 degrees counterclockwise, and stack two framed copies vertically.\n\nInput structure:\n- Blue is absent from the input.\n- One rare marker color appears in exactly two pixels.\n- The background is the unique non-marker color whose removal leaves exactly two 4-connected foreground objects, each with one marker pixel and one body color.\n- The whole grid, including background and foreground objects, is the sprite to transform.\n\nRule:\n- Rotate the entire input grid 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n- Create a blue one-pixel frame around two vertical copies of the rotated grid.\n- Use the input background color for the two endpoints of every horizontal frame/separator row.\n\nKey insight: The output is not a crop of the foreground objects; it is two copies of the complete rotated input, separated and enclosed by a newly added blue border.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"1818057f\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nDetect thin '+' shapes in a single foreground color and recolor them to teal.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- One foreground color (e.g. yellow) forms various shapes on the bg, some of which are small '+' crosses.\n\nRule:\n- A '+' center is any foreground pixel whose 4 orthogonal neighbors are also foreground.\n- Recolor each such center and its 4 neighbors to teal (8).\n- All other pixels remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight: Only compact 5-cell crosses qualify — a pixel embedded in a larger filled region also satisfies the neighbor check, so the rule highlights every local '+' motif, not just isolated ones.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6350f1f4\" lines=\"62-81\">\n<description>\nDecode a block-grid image by swapping each sprite-coded cell with a solid cell and each blank cell with the sprite.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is partitioned by black separator rows and columns into equal-size\nsquare blocks. Each block is either a copy of one two-color sprite or a\nuniform blank block of the sprite's minority color; grey pixels are erased\ncells that act as wildcards.\n\nRule\nInfer the repeated sprite from fully visible mixed-color blocks. Replace a\nblock matching that sprite, ignoring grey erasures, with a solid block of\nthe sprite's majority color. Replace a uniform blank block with the sprite.\nSeparator rows and columns, including grey erasures on them, become black.\n\nKey insight\nThe small sprite is a block-level code for one macro-cell state, and the\nuniform minority-color block is the other state; the output renders the\nopposite code in each macro-cell while cleaning erased pixels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"291dc1e1\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nReorder and stack rectangles by proximity to two colored edge lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two edge lines (h + v) meet at a corner marked by a unique color.\n- \"Blue\" line is color 1 if present, else the longer line; other is \"red.\"\n- Solid rectangles in the interior.\n\nRule:\n- Sort rectangles by (distance to blue edge, distance to red edge).\n- If the blue edge is vertical, rotate each rectangle 90° CCW.\n- Stack top-to-bottom, centered horizontally.\n\nKey insight: Edge-line colors encode a coordinate system; proximity to each axis determines stacking order and rotation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"a6953f00\" lines=\"45-63\">\n<description>\nExtract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.\n\nInput structure\n- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops\n  touching the top edge and a grid corner.\n- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.\n- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.\n\nRule\n- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to\n  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.\n- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of\n  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information\n  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8698868d\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nMatch sprites to frames by connected-hole count, then nest each sprite inside its frame.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Large hollow squares (frames) arranged in a grid; smaller filled squares (sprites) placed elsewhere.\n- Each frame and sprite has a distinct color. Sprites may contain holes (non-sprite-color pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Frames form a regular grid; some may be bg-colored (invisible).\n- Count connected hole regions inside each frame's interior and each sprite.\n- Pair each frame with the sprite sharing the same hole count.\n- Place the matched sprite inside the frame border; recolor sprite holes to the frame's color.\n\nKey insight: The matching key is the number of connected hole components, not hole area or color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2c181942\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nAttach monochrome sprites to the matching sides of a central hollow square.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A hollow NxN square (hub) with bg corners/interior and four differently-colored edges (top, bottom, left, right).\n- 2-4 monochrome sprites scattered on the grid, each matching one hub edge color.\n\nRule:\n- For each hub edge color, find the matching sprite outside the hub.\n- Rotate the sprite (0/90/180/270°) so its contact edge has exactly N-2 contiguous cells aligned with the hub side, and the rotated sprite is mirror-symmetric along the contact-edge axis (UD for left/right, LR for top/bottom).\n- Translate it to adjoin that hub side. \n- Clear everything else to bg; output is hub + attached sprites only.\n\nKey insight: Each edge color uniquely pairs a sprite to a side; symmetry along the contact-edge axis disambiguates the correct rotation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9473c6fb\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nRecolor the sparse foreground pixels in scan order along their longer spread, cycling red, teal, and grey.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Every foreground object is a single isolated 4-connected pixel.\n- The foreground pixel set has a rectangular bbox whose row or column extent defines the scan direction.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the background unchanged.\n- If the foreground bbox is at least as tall as it is wide, order foreground pixels row-major; otherwise order them column-major.\n- Recolor the ordered pixels by repeating red, teal, grey from the first pixel onward.\n\nKey insight:\n- The original foreground colors are only markers; the output color depends on each marker's ordinal position in the dominant scan direction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"252143c9\" lines=\"14-33\">\n<description>\nKeep only the center color and redraw it as a diagonal ray to its indicated corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is an odd square with a unique dominant background color.\n- The center pixel is foreground.\n- Every off-center pixel with the center color lies in the same diagonal\n  quadrant relative to the center.\n\nRule:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
